docs(doctrine): a new public package's first publish is by hand (#1226)

## Why

`docs/doctrine/promoting-private-to-public.md` §7 said a promoted
package's first publish "flows through the normal Changesets release
pipeline". Under trusted publishing that is false: npm attaches a
trusted publisher only to a name it has already seen, so the pipeline
cannot make a first publish, and since #1225 the train refuses a
never-published name before it builds anything. The crypto-openpgp
release on 2026-11-04 went the real way, by hand first.

Step 1 also set `"version": "1.0.0"`. With step 7's `major` changeset
that releases `2.0.0`, and the hand publish takes `1.0.0` without
provenance. crypto-openpgp sat at `0.0.0` on `main`, which is what the
playbook now says.

## Evidence

- §7 now gives the order that ran on 2026-11-04: hand publish of `0.0.0`
with `pnpm publish` (only pnpm rewrites `workspace:` dependencies), then
the trusted publisher, then the Version Packages PR (#1092) and the
`npm-publish` approval.
- The trusted-publisher form on npmjs.com has an "Allow npm publish"
setting (otherwise staged publishing only) and leaves a new entry
"Pending validation" with a deadline until a publish uses it; both
observed on `@motebit/crypto-openpgp` and matched against
`@motebit/verify`'s existing entry.
- `pnpm check`: all 161 drift defenses pass. Prettier clean.

## Review

- Procedure only. No change to the workflow, the guard, or who can
publish.
- `docs/ops/RUNBOOK.md` § Publish authority gains the same two npm
facts, so the doctrine and the runbook agree.

## Deferred

- Whether to deprecate `@motebit/crypto-openpgp@0.0.0` once 1.0.0 is on
npm. The founder's call.

